🥗 Using Semi-Supervised Machine Learning to Predict Food Insecurity in Low-Data Regions
A Practical Solution Toward SDG 2: Zero Hunger
📌 Introduction

Food insecurity remains a major challenge across many African regions. Governments, NGOs, and aid organizations often lack accurate, real-time data to determine which communities are most at risk. Traditional surveys are expensive, slow, and difficult to scale.

To support SDG 2 – Zero Hunger, I built a semi-supervised machine learning system that predicts food insecurity even when most regions have missing or unlabeled data. This model uses a small set of real labels combined with a large pool of unlabeled data to make accurate predictions—without requiring full datasets.

🚨 Problem Statement

Many rural communities do not have accessible or reliable data on food security status. Even when features such as rainfall, soil quality, population density, or market access are available, most regions lack a confirmed label:

Are they food secure?

Are they at risk?

This results in:

Slow decision-making

Poor targeting of food aid

High survey costs

Delayed response during crises

To achieve SDG 2.1 (“end hunger and ensure access to safe, nutritious food for all”), we need a way to predict food insecurity even with limited labels.

💡 Project Solution: Semi-Supervised Food Insecurity Prediction

I created a Python-based prediction model that uses:

✔ Label Spreading (Semi-Supervised Learning)

This algorithm learns patterns from labeled regions and spreads that knowledge to unlabeled ones.

✔ Random Forest (Self-Training)

Once the model infers high-confidence pseudo-labels, it retrains a stronger classifier using both real and pseudo data.

✔ Synthetic Dataset Generation

Because the PLP project requires functional demonstration, the script automatically generates a dataset representing 200 regions and 10 features, with 40% missing labels—mimicking real-world scarcity of labeled data.

🧠 How the Model Works
1️⃣ Generate Sample Dataset

The script creates a dataset with random features and partial labels:

Generated sample dataset at: data/regions_features.csv

2️⃣ Preprocessing

Missing values are imputed

Features are normalized

Labels are separated into labeled and unlabeled sets

3️⃣ Semi-Supervised Label Spreading

This step makes predictions on unlabeled rows and assigns pseudo-labels where confidence > 85%.

4️⃣ Self-Training Random Forest Classifier

The final model combines real labels + high-quality pseudo-labels to learn a stronger representation.

5️⃣ Evaluation

The model is tested on unseen labeled data and reports metrics like F1-score, accuracy, recall, and ROC-AUC.

